\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\looseness=-1 We evaluate the pipeline on two LIBERO task suites~\cite{liu2023libero}, asking: (i)~can a cheap uncertainty probe enable adaptive compute allocation? (ii)~what is the relative contribution of selection vs.\ correction? (iii)~how does conditioning interact with magnitude? (iv)~where does the pipeline fail?

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

\paragraph{Benchmarks and base VLA.}
We use LIBERO-Object (10 tasks, novel object instances) and LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks, spatial reasoning). Each task is evaluated over 10 rollouts (max 300 steps); success is binary.
We build on SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (450M parameters, flow matching), using dual-camera RGB ($256{\times}256$) and 8D proprioceptive state. Actions are 7D (6D pose + 1D gripper); the gripper is thresholded outside the pipeline.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
All use the same frozen SmolVLA: \textbf{VLA-only} ($K{=}1$, no correction), \textbf{Random} ($K{=}5{/}10$, uniform sample, no CRM), and \textbf{Argmin} ($K{=}5{/}10$, $L_2$ selection, no CRM).

\paragraph{Training and statistics.}
CRM is trained on 26{,}737 samples (10 demos $\times$ 20 tasks, $K{=}10$ forward passes each), Adam $\mathrm{lr}{=}10^{-3}$, batch 32, 50 epochs, $\varepsilon{=}0.2$. All evaluation tasks are covered by CRM training data (in-distribution).
We report seed-level Mann-Whitney U tests (two-sided); 3--7 seeds per condition, none discarded.

\subsection{Adaptive Inference-Time Compute}
\label{sec:adaptive_k}

The key question is whether compute can be allocated adaptively per step rather than using a fixed $K$ for all steps. We validate the $K{=}2$ $L_2$ probe (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k_method}) and evaluate threshold-based adaptive allocation.

\paragraph{Uncertainty signal validation.}
The pairwise $L_2$ distance from $K{=}2$ samples correlates near-perfectly with the full $K{=}10$ sample variance ($\rho{=}0.9979$, Spearman; Figure~\ref{fig:k2_correlation}). Three alternative signals fail: flow-matching velocity divergence (FMVD, $\rho{=}0.08$), CRM correction magnitude at $K{=}1$ ($\rho{=}0.15$), and CRM correction magnitude at $K{=}2$ ($\rho{=}0.13$). The $K{=}2$ $L_2$ distance is the only viable per-step uncertainty proxy among the four candidates tested.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig5_k2_correlation.pdf}
\caption{Per-step uncertainty signals vs.\ $K{=}10$ sample variance. (a)~$K{=}2$ $L_2$ pairwise distance achieves $\rho{=}0.9979$, validating it as a near-perfect proxy. (b)~FMVD ($\rho{=}0.08$) shows no correlation, confirming that ODE-space signals do not predict action-space variance.}
\label{fig:k2_correlation}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Threshold sweep (variable-$K$ protocol).}
To validate the $K{=}2$ probe as an uncertainty signal independently of Plan~A, we test a variable-$K$ protocol that expands to $K{=}10$ on high-uncertainty steps (details in Appendix~\ref{sec:spatial_adaptive}).
\looseness=-1 Table~\ref{tab:adaptive} reports performance on LIBERO-Object. Among variable-$K$ thresholds, P50 achieves the best trade-off (91\% of $K{=}10$ SR at 38\% compute). However, Plan~A ($K{=}2$ selective CRM) Pareto-dominates all thresholds: $18.3$\% at avg\_$K{=}2$, exceeding fixed $K{=}10$ ($16.8$\%) with 80\% fewer VLA forward passes. The sweep confirms the $K{=}2$ signal is effective, but selective CRM is its optimal utilization.
\looseness=-1 The optimal threshold shifts with task difficulty: on Spatial (easier), P90 reaches 33.0\% at avg\_$K{=}3.55$ while P25 drops to 31.7\%; on Object (harder) P50 is the sweet spot (Appendix~\ref{sec:spatial_adaptive}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Adaptive $K$ threshold sweep and Plan~A on LIBERO-Object ($n{=}3$ seeds unless noted). Thresholds $\tau$ on training-set $L_2$ distance: P90${=}$0.127, P75${=}$0.099, P50${=}$0.075, P25${=}$0.055. Plan~A Pareto-dominates all variable-$K$ thresholds. $^\S$\,$n{=}6$. $^\|$\,$n{=}8$.}
\label{tab:adaptive}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
$\tau$ & SR (\%) & avg\_$K$ & fast path (\%) & SR/SR$_{K{=}10}$ \\
\midrule
\textbf{Plan~A (ours)} & $\mathbf{18.3 \pm 2.1}$ & \textbf{2.0} & \textbf{16.3} & \textbf{109\%} \\
\midrule
P90 & $13.3 \pm 1.2$ & 2.62 & 92.2 & 79\% \\
P75 & $13.7 \pm 2.1$ & 3.08 & 86.5 & 82\% \\
P50 & $15.3 \pm 1.2$ & 3.81 & 77.3 & 91\% \\
P25 & $14.3 \pm 1.2$ & 4.79 & 65.1 & 85\% \\
\midrule
Fixed $K{=}10^\S$ & $16.8 \pm 1.8$ & 10.0 & 0 & 100\% \\
VLA-only$^{\|}$ & $11.0 \pm 1.3$ & 1.0 & --- & 65\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Main Results and Component Decomposition}
\label{sec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} compares Plan~A (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k_method}) with analysis baselines that isolate the contribution of selection and correction at fixed $K$.

\paragraph{Plan~A: selective correction at $K{=}2$.}
\looseness=-1 The proposed pipeline achieves $18.3 \pm 2.1$\% on Object (3 seeds) and $35.0 \pm 1.0$\% on Spatial, exceeding CRM $K{=}10$ ($16.8$\%, $34.4$\%) with 80\% fewer VLA forward passes and matching CRM $K{=}20$ ($18.8$\%, $34.3$\%) at 90\% fewer (Appendix~\ref{sec:k_scaling}). The CRM trigger rate is task-dependent: 83.7\% on Object (16.3\% fast-path steps) and 97.6\% on Spatial, where correction is almost universally beneficial. Selective application outperforms unconditional correction at $K{=}10$, consistent with over-triggering degrading performance (P25 $<$ P50, Table~\ref{tab:adaptive}). With $K{=}2$, the centroid has lower variance than at $K{=}10$, providing a cleaner starting point for bounded correction.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Task success rate (\%\,$\uparrow$) on LIBERO-Object and LIBERO-Spatial. Mean $\pm$ std; $n$: Object seeds. Spatial $n$: 5 (VLA-only), 4 ($K{=}5$), 3 (Random $K{=}10$, CRM $K{=}10$, Plan~A). $\Delta$: vs.\ VLA-only. Top: proposed method (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k_method}); middle/bottom: analysis baselines.}
\label{tab:main}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Method & $K$ & Object (\%) & $n$ & Spatial (\%) & $\Delta$ Obj \\
\midrule
\textbf{Plan~A (ours)} & \textbf{2} & $\mathbf{18.3 \pm 2.1}$ & \textbf{3} & $\mathbf{35.0 \pm 1.0}$ & $\mathbf{+7.3}$ \\
\midrule
VLA-only & 1 & $11.0 \pm 1.3$ & 8 & $29.4 \pm 1.5$ & --- \\
Random & 5 & $10.5 \pm 2.9$ & 4 & --- & $-0.5$ \\
Argmin & 5 & $13.0 \pm 1.6$ & 4 & --- & $+2.0$ \\
CRM obs\_only & 5 & $16.0 \pm 2.2$ & 7 & $34.5 \pm 2.1$ & $+5.0$ \\
\midrule
Random & 10 & $9.7 \pm 1.5$ & 6 & $29.3 \pm 4.2$ & $-1.3$ \\
Argmin & 10 & $12.3 \pm 2.6$ & 6 & --- & $+1.3$ \\
CRM obs\_only & 10 & $16.8 \pm 1.8$ & 6 & $34.4 \pm 1.5$ & $+5.8$ \\
\midrule
CRM perdof & 10 & $11.0 \pm 1.8$ & 6 & --- & $\phantom{+}0.0$ \\
CRM scalar & 10 & $\phantom{0}8.5 \pm 1.5$ & 4 & --- & $-2.5$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Component decomposition (fixed-$K$ analysis).}
\looseness=-2 The remaining rows of Table~\ref{tab:main} are analysis baselines at fixed $K$, not proposed methods. CRM obs\_only at $K{=}10$ reaches $16.8 \pm 1.8$\% on Object ($p{=}0.003$) and $34.4 \pm 1.5$\% on Spatial. Random selection fails to exceed VLA-only on either benchmark (Object $9.7$\% vs.\ $11.0$\%; Spatial $29.3$\% vs.\ $29.4$\%), confirming that sampling more actions without ranking is insufficient; argmin adds $+$1.3\,pp, and CRM correction contributes $+$4.5\,pp (78\% of the gain). At $K{=}5$, correction contributes 55\%. At $K{=}1$, correction without centering falls below baseline ($10.3$\%), validating the $K{\geq}2$ requirement.

\vspace{-2pt}
\subsection{Conditioning, Magnitude, and Distribution Shift}
\label{sec:magnitude_results}
\label{sec:failure_modes}

\looseness=-2 The CRM conditioning interacts with $\varepsilon$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:magnitude_appendix}): obs\_only is stable (12.5--16.8\%); perdof collapses (${\sim}$14\% $\to$ 0\%) because $K{=}10$ in $d{=}7$ yields effectively low-rank covariance and large $\varepsilon$ amplifies noisy per-DOF estimates; scalar (8.5\%) falls below VLA-only. Three distribution shifts define further boundaries: (1)~input ($K{=}1$, 10.3\%); (2)~task (near-OOD $-$12\,pp, far-OOD neutral $p{=}0.46$); (3)~feature (perdof $\to$ 0\%). All share a mechanism: CRM degrades when inputs depart from training distribution (Appendix~\ref{sec:per_task}). Plan~A runs 1.82$\times$ faster than $K{=}10$ with 0.28\,ms CRM overhead (Appendix~\ref{sec:latency}).

We further validate cross-VLA generalization on $\pi_{0.5}$ (3.6B)~\cite{black2024pi0}; see Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_vla}.

