\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\looseness=-1 We evaluate ACR-VLA on three frozen flow-matching VLAs spanning $0.4$B to $7$B parameters across four LIBERO suites, asking: (i)~does the pipeline transfer across VLA scale and task family? (ii)~is the $K{=}2$ uncertainty probe a faithful proxy for full sample variance? (iii)~what is the relative contribution of selection vs.\ correction, and where does the pipeline fail?

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

\paragraph{Benchmarks.}
\looseness=-1 LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero} provides four manipulation suites of 10 tasks each that target distinct distribution shifts: \textbf{Object} (novel object instances), \textbf{Spatial} (novel scene layouts), \textbf{Goal} (novel task objectives with familiar objects), and \textbf{Long} (long-horizon multi-step tasks). We evaluate 10 rollouts per task (max 300 steps per episode) and report binary success.

\paragraph{VLAs under test.}
\looseness=-1 We apply the identical pipeline to three frozen flow-matching VLAs covering an order of magnitude in scale. \textbf{SmolVLA}~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} ($0.4$B) uses dual-camera RGB ($256{\times}256$), 8D proprioception, and produces 7D actions (6D pose plus 1D gripper, thresholded outside the pipeline). \textbf{$\pi_{0.5}$}~\cite{black2024pi0} ($3.6$B, PaliGemma2 vision backbone with Gemma2 language model) emits 50-step action chunks with a 2048D observation feature. \textbf{OpenVLA-OFT}~\cite{kim2024openvla} ($7$B, flow-matching head) tests the applicability boundary: at the temperature setting we adopt ($T{=}0.5$) it is nearly deterministic, so sample diversity vanishes and the uncertainty signal is degraded.

\paragraph{Correction Residual Module.}
\looseness=-1 A unified CRM is trained per VLA on the combined LIBERO data (40 tasks, 10 per suite, 10 demonstrations each, $K{=}10$ forward passes per step) so that all four evaluation suites are in-distribution. The architecture is fixed at three fully connected layers ($\sim$327K parameters); only the input projection adapts to the VLA's observation dimension. Training uses Adam ($\mathrm{lr}{=}10^{-3}$), batch 32, 50 epochs, and norm bound $\varepsilon{=}0.2$. The uncertainty threshold $\tau$ is set to the median of the training-set $L_2$ distribution.

\paragraph{Baselines and statistics.}
\looseness=-1 All baselines share the frozen VLA: \textbf{VLA-only} ($K{=}1$, no correction); \textbf{Random} ($K{\in}\{5,10\}$, uniform sample, no CRM); \textbf{Argmin} ($K{\in}\{5,10\}$, $L_2$-to-centroid selection, no CRM); \textbf{CRM (unconditional)} ($K{\in}\{5,10\}$, argmin selection plus CRM applied at every step). Statistical significance uses seed-level two-sided Mann-Whitney U tests with 3 to 9 seeds per condition (none discarded).

\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

\looseness=-1 Table~\ref{tab:main} reports the central comparison: VLA-only vs.\ ACR-VLA (Plan~A, $K{=}2$ selective correction) on each VLA across the four LIBERO suites. On SmolVLA, Plan~A reaches $18.3 \pm 2.1$\% on Object ($+7.5$\,pp, $p{=}0.003$) and $35.0 \pm 1.0$\% on Spatial ($+5.6$\,pp), exceeding the fixed-$K{=}10$ unconditional CRM ($16.8$\% Object, $34.4$\% Spatial) while issuing 80\% fewer VLA forward passes and matching $K{=}20$ ($18.8$\%) at 90\% fewer (Appendix~\ref{sec:k_scaling}). The trigger rate confirms compute is allocated where it pays: 83.7\% of Object steps invoke the CRM (16.3\% fast-path), rising to 97.6\% on Spatial (2.4\% fast-path), where correction is almost universally beneficial.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Task success rate (\%\,$\uparrow$) on the four LIBERO suites for three frozen flow-matching VLAs. Plan~A is ACR-VLA with $K{=}2$ selective correction (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k_method}). Means $\pm$ std over seed-level rollouts; sample sizes range from 3 to 9 depending on condition. Pending evaluations are marked \placeholder{}.}
\label{tab:main}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{6pt}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Method & $K$ & Object & Spatial & Goal & Long \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{6}{l}{\textit{SmolVLA ($0.4$B, flow-matching)}} \\
VLA-only             & 1 & $10.8 \pm 1.3$          & $29.4 \pm 1.5$          & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} \\
\textbf{Plan~A (ours)} & \textbf{2} & $\mathbf{18.3 \pm 2.1}$ & $\mathbf{35.0 \pm 1.0}$ & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{6}{l}{\textit{$\pi_{0.5}$ ($3.6$B, flow-matching)}} \\
VLA-only             & 1 & $81.5 \pm 0.5$ & $84.2 \pm 1.0$ & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} \\
\textbf{Plan~A (ours)} & \textbf{2} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{6}{l}{\textit{OpenVLA-OFT ($7$B, flow-matching, $T{=}0.5$)}} \\
VLA-only             & 1 & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} \\
\textbf{Plan~A (ours)} & \textbf{2} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\looseness=-1 The remaining cells of Table~\ref{tab:main} probe two questions the pipeline must answer to claim generality. $\pi_{0.5}$ starts from a strong baseline ($81.5$/$84.2$\% on Object/Spatial), leaving limited headroom; whether bounded residual correction still adds value when the VLA is already accurate tests the method outside the high-error regime where every prior result was collected. OpenVLA-OFT, by contrast, is nearly deterministic at $T{=}0.5$, so the $K{=}2$ pairwise distance collapses and the uncertainty gate degenerates. This condition stress-tests the central premise of the pipeline (stochastic diversity) and is reported as a boundary case rather than a target operating point (Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}). Goal and Long evaluations are in progress for all three VLAs; the unified CRM is trained on the combined data, so the architecture is unchanged.

\subsection{Adaptive Inference-Time Compute}
\label{sec:adaptive_k}

\looseness=-1 Plan~A allocates compute via a learned threshold on a $K{=}2$ uncertainty probe. Two claims underpin the design: (a)~the $K{=}2$ pairwise $L_2$ distance is a faithful proxy for the full $K{=}10$ sample variance, and (b)~selective correction Pareto-dominates the unconditional pipeline at any fixed-$K$ budget. We validate both.

\paragraph{Uncertainty signal.}
\looseness=-1 The $K{=}2$ pairwise $L_2$ distance achieves Spearman $\rho{=}0.9979$ against the $K{=}10$ sample variance (Figure~\ref{fig:k2_correlation}). Three alternative signals fail: flow-matching velocity divergence (FMVD, $\rho{=}0.08$), CRM correction magnitude at $K{=}1$ ($\rho{=}0.15$), and CRM correction magnitude at $K{=}2$ ($\rho{=}0.13$). The pairwise $L_2$ is the only viable per-step uncertainty proxy among the four candidates, and the $K{=}2$ probe is computationally free under batched inference (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k_method}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig5_k2_correlation.pdf}
\caption{Per-step uncertainty signals vs.\ $K{=}10$ sample variance. (a)~$K{=}2$ pairwise $L_2$ distance reaches $\rho{=}0.9979$, validating it as a near-perfect proxy. (b)~FMVD ($\rho{=}0.08$) shows no correlation, confirming that ODE-space signals do not predict action-space variance.}
\label{fig:k2_correlation}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Threshold sweep.}
\looseness=-1 To verify that the signal is useful independently of Plan~A, Table~\ref{tab:adaptive} reports a variable-$K$ protocol on LIBERO-Object that expands to $K{=}10$ on steps where the probe exceeds threshold (P90, P75, P50, P25 of the training $L_2$ distribution). Among the variable-$K$ thresholds, P50 achieves the best trade-off (91\% of $K{=}10$ success rate at 38\% of the compute). Plan~A Pareto-dominates all of them: $18.3$\% at avg\_$K{=}2.0$ exceeds the best variable-$K$ point ($15.3$\% at avg\_$K{=}3.81$) and the fixed-$K{=}10$ pipeline ($16.8$\%). The probe is a useful uncertainty signal; selective correction is its optimal utilization. The optimal threshold shifts with task difficulty: on Spatial (easier) P90 is the sweet spot, while on Object (harder) it is P50 (Appendix~\ref{sec:spatial_adaptive}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Adaptive-$K$ threshold sweep and Plan~A on LIBERO-Object. Thresholds $\tau$ are percentiles of the training-set $L_2$ distance (P90${=}$0.127, P75${=}$0.099, P50${=}$0.075, P25${=}$0.055). Plan~A Pareto-dominates all variable-$K$ thresholds. $n{=}3$ unless noted. $^\S$\,$n{=}6$. $^\|$\,$n{=}9$.}
\label{tab:adaptive}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
$\tau$ & SR (\%) & avg\_$K$ & fast path (\%) & SR/SR$_{K{=}10}$ \\
\midrule
\textbf{Plan~A (ours)} & $\mathbf{18.3 \pm 2.1}$ & \textbf{2.0} & \textbf{16.3} & \textbf{109\%} \\
\midrule
P90 & $13.3 \pm 1.2$ & 2.62 & 92.2 & 79\% \\
P75 & $13.7 \pm 2.1$ & 3.08 & 86.5 & 82\% \\
P50 & $15.3 \pm 1.2$ & 3.81 & 77.3 & 91\% \\
P25 & $14.3 \pm 1.2$ & 4.79 & 65.1 & 85\% \\
\midrule
Fixed $K{=}10^\S$ & $16.8 \pm 1.8$ & 10.0 & 0 & 100\% \\
VLA-only$^\|$     & $10.8 \pm 1.3$ & 1.0  & --- & 64\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Component Analysis}
\label{sec:magnitude_results}
\label{sec:failure_modes}

\looseness=-1 The Plan~A pipeline composes three operations: multi-sampling, argmin selection, and bounded CRM correction. To isolate each contribution we report fixed-$K$ baselines that strip operations one at a time (Table~\ref{tab:components}, SmolVLA). Random selection at $K{=}10$ \emph{fails} to exceed VLA-only on both benchmarks ($9.7$\% vs.\ $10.8$\% on Object; $29.3$\% vs.\ $29.4$\% on Spatial), so additional sampling without ranking is not enough. Argmin selection adds $+1.5$\,pp on Object (from $10.8$\% to $12.3$\%), and CRM correction adds another $+4.5$\,pp (to $16.8$\%). Learned correction contributes 68\% of the gain at $K{=}5$ and is the near-total contributor at $K{=}10$; this finding motivated the adaptive design, since scaling samples beyond $K{=}2$ buys little once correction is in place.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Component decomposition on SmolVLA. Random$\to$Argmin isolates the value of selection; Argmin$\to$CRM isolates the value of correction. Conditioning variants at $K{=}10$ and the $K{=}1$ CRM probe the failure modes that motivated the design choices. $n$ ranges from 3 to 9.}
\label{tab:components}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{6pt}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Method & $K$ & Object (\%) & Spatial (\%) & $\Delta$ Obj & $n$ \\
\midrule
VLA-only             & 1  & $10.8 \pm 1.3$ & $29.4 \pm 1.5$ & ---       & 9 \\
\midrule
Random               & 5  & $10.5 \pm 2.9$ & \placeholder{} & $-0.3$    & 4 \\
Argmin               & 5  & $13.0 \pm 1.6$ & \placeholder{} & $+2.2$    & 4 \\
CRM (obs\_only)      & 5  & $16.0 \pm 2.2$ & $34.5 \pm 2.1$ & $+5.2$    & 7 \\
\midrule
Random               & 10 & $\phantom{0}9.7 \pm 1.5$ & $29.3 \pm 4.2$ & $-1.1$ & 6 \\
Argmin               & 10 & $12.3 \pm 2.6$ & \placeholder{} & $+1.5$    & 6 \\
CRM (obs\_only)      & 10 & $16.8 \pm 1.8$ & $34.4 \pm 1.5$ & $+6.0$    & 6 \\
\midrule
CRM (perdof)         & 10 & $11.0 \pm 1.8$ & \placeholder{} & $+0.2$    & 6 \\
CRM (scalar)         & 10 & $\phantom{0}8.5 \pm 1.5$ & \placeholder{} & $-2.3$ & 4 \\
CRM (K{=}1, no centering) & 1 & $10.3 \pm 1.5$ & \placeholder{} & $-0.5$ & 3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Why $K{\geq}2$.}
\looseness=-1 Applying the CRM directly to a single sample ($K{=}1$, no centering) yields $10.3 \pm 1.5$\% on Object, below the VLA-only baseline of $10.8$\%. The correction is well-defined only on a centered input: with a single sample the residual is regressed onto a noisy origin and amplifies rather than corrects. This degenerate condition is what the $K{=}2$ probe was designed to avoid: the centroid provides a stable anchor at minimal additional cost, and the pairwise distance simultaneously serves as the uncertainty signal.

\paragraph{Conditioning and magnitude.}
\looseness=-1 The CRM conditioning interacts with the norm bound $\varepsilon$. The \emph{obs\_only} variant is stable across $\varepsilon \in [0.05, 0.2]$ (12.5\% to 16.8\% on Object). The \emph{perdof} variant injects conformal radii and per-DOF standard deviations and collapses from $\sim$14\% at $\varepsilon{=}0.05$ to $0$\% at $\varepsilon{=}0.5$: the $K{=}10$ covariance is effectively low-rank in $d{=}7$, and large $\varepsilon$ amplifies noisy per-DOF estimates. The \emph{scalar} variant falls below baseline ($8.5$\%). Conformal features therefore fail to improve closed-loop performance, motivating obs\_only as the primary CRM (full sweep in Appendix~\ref{sec:magnitude_appendix}).

\paragraph{Distribution shift boundaries.}
\looseness=-1 Three distribution shifts define the operating envelope. \textbf{Input shift} ($K{=}1$): correction without centering degrades performance, as above. \textbf{Task shift}: near-OOD tasks drop $-12$\,pp under the CRM, while far-OOD tasks are neutral ($p{=}0.46$), consistent with the CRM transferring on familiar dynamics but degrading on subtly different ones (Appendix~\ref{sec:per_task}). \textbf{Feature shift}: perdof collapses to $0$\% as noted above. All three share a single mechanism: the CRM degrades when inputs depart from the training distribution. The Plan~A pipeline runs $1.82\times$ faster than fixed $K{=}10$ at the same compute budget, with batched $K{=}2$ inference at $\sim$170\,ms per step and CRM overhead of $0.28$\,ms (Appendix~\ref{sec:latency}).

\subsection{Cross-VLA Generalization}

\looseness=-1 The pipeline is VLA-agnostic by construction: the $K{=}2$ probe, argmin selection, and bounded CRM correction depend only on the VLA producing diverse action samples and exposing an observation embedding. Across the three VLAs in Table~\ref{tab:main}, only the CRM input projection adapts to the observation dimension; the architecture, training recipe, and threshold percentile are unchanged. Two open questions guide the remaining evaluations. Does correction help when the baseline is already strong (the $\pi_{0.5}$ headroom regime, $81$ to $84$\% on Object/Spatial)? And does the uncertainty gate degrade gracefully when sample diversity vanishes (the OpenVLA-OFT near-deterministic regime)? Cross-VLA results are reported in Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_vla} alongside the per-VLA threshold-sensitivity analysis.

